WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.800 position:63%
The Starry Night is one of the most&nbsp;
famous paintings in history. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.300 position:63%
for one hundred and thirty-seven years, it held&nbsp;
a mystery that no one had been able to solve.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.800 position:63%
For generations, historians and astronomers&nbsp;
have wondered whether the sky we see in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.600 position:63%
work really existed. Whether the swirls,&nbsp;
the blazing stars, and that moon shining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.700 position:63%
in the upper right corner were simply a&nbsp;
product of Vincent van Gogh's imagination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.400 position:63%
or whether he truly painted what&nbsp;
was right in front of his eyes.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.900 position:63%
But just a month ago, a group&nbsp;
of astronomers from South Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.300 position:63%
published a study that could completely&nbsp;
change the way we look at this painting.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.200 position:63%
Because, according to them, that sky really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.700 position:63%
did exist. And that could give this&nbsp;
masterpiece a completely new meaning.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.700 position:63%
But to understand what this whole mystery is&nbsp;
about, we have to go back to eighteen eighty-nine.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:58.000 position:63%
In May of that year, after one of the worst crises&nbsp;
of his life, Vincent van Gogh voluntarily admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.200 position:63%
himself to the asylum of Saint-Paul-de-Mausole,&nbsp;
in Saint-Rémy-de-Provence, in the south of France.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.700 position:63%
We have to keep in mind that Vincent was not&nbsp;
allowed to paint in his room. But from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.800 position:63%
east-facing window, he could see the fields, the&nbsp;
hills, and the sky. There, he made sketches in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.600 position:63%
ink or charcoal, and then, during the day, he&nbsp;
worked in a small studio on the ground floor.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.100 position:63%
And it was precisely under these&nbsp;
circumstances that The Starry Night was born.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.600 position:63%
For a long time, historians had dated it to&nbsp;
mid-June of eighteen eighty-nine, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:36.100 position:63%
there is a letter Van Gogh wrote to his brother&nbsp;
Theo around June eighteenth, in which he told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:41.800 position:63%
him that he finally had a landscape with olive&nbsp;
trees, and also a new study of a starry sky.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.600 position:63%
Until just a month ago, this was&nbsp;
the most widely accepted theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.600 position:63%
since everything seemed to&nbsp;
fit. But there was a problem.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.000 position:63%
If we look at the upper right corner&nbsp;
of the painting, we see a very thin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.500 position:63%
sickle-shaped moon, in its waning phase…&nbsp;
And this is where the mystery begins.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:04.193 position:63%
According to astronomical calculations,&nbsp;
in mid-June of eighteen eighty-nine, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.193 --> 00:02:08.293 position:63%
moon was full or nearly full.&nbsp;
And even around June nineteenth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.293 --> 00:02:13.493 position:63%
more than half of its face was still lit. In&nbsp;
other words, the sky in the painting did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:17.993 position:63%
match the sky Van Gogh would have seen&nbsp;
on the dates he supposedly painted it.

00:02:17.993 --> 00:02:23.193 position:63%
And that left two possibilities. Either&nbsp;
Van Gogh had invented that sky entirely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.193 --> 00:02:25.793 position:63%
or the story we knew about&nbsp;
this painting was wrong.

00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:31.893 position:63%
The man behind this research is Elijah&nbsp;
Jeong-hyun Kim, a South Korean astronomer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.893 --> 00:02:36.993 position:63%
head of a telescope company in Seoul,&nbsp;
and a researcher at Kyung Hee University.

00:02:36.993 --> 00:02:43.493 position:63%
His obsession with this painting led him to travel&nbsp;
to Saint-Rémy in twenty nineteen, twice. The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.493 --> 00:02:49.093 position:63%
time in mid-June, and the second around July&nbsp;
twenty-second. He wanted to see with his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.093 --> 00:02:55.093 position:63%
eyes the same sky Van Gogh would have seen. And&nbsp;
on this trip, he discovered something revealing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.093 --> 00:02:59.393 position:63%
in the early hours of July, bright stars&nbsp;
appeared that were not there in June.

00:02:59.893 --> 00:03:03.393 position:63%
From there, his team reconstructed&nbsp;
the exact sky that could be seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.393 --> 00:03:08.393 position:63%
from the asylum window in June and July of&nbsp;
eighteen eighty-nine, and searched for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.393 --> 00:03:13.893 position:63%
three most important elements that appear&nbsp;
together in the painting: a waning moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:18.693 position:63%
the planet Venus, and a group of stars&nbsp;
scattered across the left side of the sky.

00:03:18.693 --> 00:03:22.693 position:63%
That group of stars, according&nbsp;
to Kim, is not an invention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.693 --> 00:03:28.293 position:63%
but a real star cluster called the Hyades,&nbsp;
in the constellation Taurus. To prove it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.293 --> 00:03:33.193 position:63%
they mathematically compared the shape of&nbsp;
five stars in the painting with the real ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.193 --> 00:03:37.893 position:63%
and the match was even stronger in a pen&nbsp;
drawing Van Gogh made of the same scene.

00:03:37.893 --> 00:03:42.393 position:63%
The result of all this was surprising,&nbsp;
as it shows that in June and July of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.393 --> 00:03:47.793 position:63%
eighteen eighty-nine, there were only three early&nbsp;
mornings when the moon appeared above Venus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:51.993 position:63%
just like in the painting: June&nbsp;
twenty-third, July twenty-second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.993 --> 00:03:57.193 position:63%
and July twenty-third. But only the&nbsp;
ones in July also showed the Hyades.

00:03:57.193 --> 00:03:59.993 position:63%
And of all of them, one fit better than any other.

00:04:00.793 --> 00:04:04.694 position:63%
But before we continue, if you'd like to&nbsp;
have this painting in your collection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.694 --> 00:04:07.294 position:63%
you can get it through the&nbsp;
link in the description.

00:04:07.294 --> 00:04:11.094 position:63%
In our catalog, you'll find more than&nbsp;
one thousand works, so you can choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.094 --> 00:04:16.394 position:63%
your favorites and start your very own art&nbsp;
collection at home. We ship worldwide for free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.394 --> 00:04:20.894 position:63%
and every purchase helps us keep bringing&nbsp;
you videos like this one every week.

00:04:20.894 --> 00:04:22.294 position:63%
Now, let's continue.

00:04:22.794 --> 00:04:24.994 position:63%
According to the study I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.994 --> 00:04:28.894 position:63%
the sky in The Starry Night matches the&nbsp;
one that could be seen from Saint-Rémy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.894 --> 00:04:33.394 position:63%
at around three in the morning on July&nbsp;
twenty-second, eighteen eighty-nine.

00:04:33.394 --> 00:04:37.994 position:63%
That night, the waning moon, Venus,&nbsp;
and the Hyades came together in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.994 --> 00:04:43.594 position:63%
same small portion of the sky, right in the&nbsp;
direction Van Gogh's window was facing. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.594 --> 00:04:48.194 position:63%
according to their weather analysis, the&nbsp;
sky was clear enough for him to see it.

00:04:48.194 --> 00:04:53.194 position:63%
But then, what about the letter Vincent&nbsp;
wrote to his brother on June eighteenth?

00:04:53.194 --> 00:04:57.794 position:63%
The team's proposal is that Van Gogh came up&nbsp;
with the idea of painting the night from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.794 --> 00:05:02.394 position:63%
room in June, but finished the painting&nbsp;
after that July twenty-second. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.394 --> 00:05:06.894 position:63%
are details that support this idea. Some&nbsp;
historians had already pointed out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.894 --> 00:05:11.594 position:63%
The Starry Night is stylistically different&nbsp;
from the other works he painted that June.

00:05:11.594 --> 00:05:17.394 position:63%
What's more, in a letter written between late&nbsp;
May and early June, Van Gogh himself told his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.394 --> 00:05:23.294 position:63%
brother that, that morning, he had seen the&nbsp;
countryside from his window long before sunrise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.294 --> 00:05:28.094 position:63%
with nothing but the morning star, which&nbsp;
looked very big. That morning star was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:33.794 position:63%
Venus. In other words, Van Gogh watched&nbsp;
the sky before dawn, again and again.

00:05:33.794 --> 00:05:37.794 position:63%
And there is one detail that makes&nbsp;
all of this even more chilling. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.794 --> 00:05:43.594 position:63%
is believed that right around those dates&nbsp;
in July, Van Gogh suffered a new crisis.

00:05:43.594 --> 00:05:47.294 position:63%
As with any great discovery,&nbsp;
not all experts are convinced.

00:05:47.294 --> 00:05:52.894 position:63%
Some astronomers, like Bradley Schaefer, argue&nbsp;
that the position and orientation of the stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.894 --> 00:05:56.894 position:63%
don't quite match the painting. Others&nbsp;
point out that the June eighteenth letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.894 --> 00:06:02.694 position:63%
is a test this new theory still has to pass.&nbsp;
And there's even a rather interesting theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.694 --> 00:06:08.695 position:63%
from physicist José Luis Aragón: since Van&nbsp;
Gogh painted from sketches and from memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.696 --> 00:06:11.896 position:63%
the sky could be a combination of several nights.

00:06:11.896 --> 00:06:16.896 position:63%
But even Aragón acknowledges that the study's&nbsp;
message is convincing enough to suggest that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.896 --> 00:06:22.696 position:63%
this painting is not pure fantasy, because Van&nbsp;
Gogh watched the real sky with great attention.

00:06:23.296 --> 00:06:27.196 position:63%
What is undeniable is that for one&nbsp;
hundred and thirty-seven years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.196 --> 00:06:32.896 position:63%
we thought The Starry Night was an imaginary&nbsp;
sky from Van Gogh's mind. And perhaps, in part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.896 --> 00:06:37.696 position:63%
it is. But now we know that behind&nbsp;
those swirls there was something real:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.696 --> 00:06:41.896 position:63%
a man awake in the middle of the night,&nbsp;
looking out the window of an asylum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.896 --> 00:06:47.296 position:63%
while the moon, Venus, and a small group&nbsp;
of stars rose above the hills of Provence.

00:06:48.096 --> 00:06:52.396 position:63%
I'd love to know what you think about all this.&nbsp;
Do you believe Van Gogh could have painted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.396 --> 00:06:58.096 position:63%
sky he saw that July morning? Or is The Starry&nbsp;
Night, as we've believed all this time, nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.096 --> 00:07:03.296 position:63%
more than a scene born from his imagination?&nbsp;
I'll be reading your thoughts in the comments.

00:07:03.296 --> 00:07:07.796 position:63%
Thank you for staying with me until the end of&nbsp;
this video, and I'll see you in the next one.

